No—the original loop is incorrect because a failed comparison updates expected but leaves desired stale. For example, A and B both start with expected = 0 and desired = 1; A succeeds, setting the counter to 1, then B fails and receives expected = 1. B can then successfully replace 1 with its stale desired = 1, so both calls return but the final count is only 1.

The retry invariant in the newly written implementation below is that, immediately before every comparison, desired equals expected + 1. A failed comparison updates expected to the value observed by that attempt, and the next iteration recomputes desired; this also handles spurious failure, even when expected remains unchanged. A successful comparison therefore increments the value it actually matched, with unsigned arithmetic wrapping modulo 2^64; this test stays well below that limit. Relaxed ordering suffices for this standalone counter, and joining all workers before checking ensures the test observes their completed increments; this is a fresh complete implementation, not a claim about previously displayed source.

